l\": \"https://ehaweb.org/about-eha/news-updates/ehas-long-term-advocacy-for-hematology-achieves-a-major-milestone\", \"text\": \"Tuesday, September 29, 2026 EHA's Long-Term Advocacy for Hematology Achieves a Major Milestone\", \"source_region\": \"main\", \"source_context\": {\"start\": 4559, \"end\": 4739, \"text\": \"rants\\nSpecialized Working Groups\\nWhat’s new?\\nGo to overview\\nTuesday, September 29, 2026\\nEHA's Long-Term Advocacy for Hematology Achieves a Major Milestone\\nTuesday, September 22, 20\"}}], \"supported_parent_doc_ids\": [], \"source_pages\": [{\"doc_id\": \"web-e3399519f4f6cb7b4402f64f\", \"title\": \"SWG Grants - The European Hematology Association (EHA)SWG Grants - The European Hematology Association (EHA)\", \"source_ranges\": [{\"start\": 3923, \"end\": 3978, \"excerpt_start\": 0, \"excerpt_end\": 55}, {\"start\": 4262, \"end\": 5807, \"excerpt_start\": 56, \"excerpt_end\": 1601}], \"navigation_excerpt\": true, \"text\": \"Left Sidebar\\n3 min read · May 1, 2025\\nMay 1, 2025\\nShare\\nSWG Grants\\nThe call for applications is closed. The next call will be open in 2027.\\nWinners will be announced by the end of December 2026.\\nThese grants are intended to support small, collaborative SWG-led research projects that strengthen scientific exchange, increase member engagement, and build lasting research networks across the EHA community.\\nEvery year, we award grants to projects that will:\\nHelp us build a collaborative network\\nIncrease engagement within SWGs\\nWhat an SWG Grant can cover\\nSWG Grants can cover costs related to:\\nData management\\nData analysis\\nSample collections and storage\\nThey cannot be used to:\\nSupport fellowships\\nPay individual salaries\\nPurchase substantial equipment\\nHow many grants will be avarded per this funding cycle?\\nUp to 5 SWG grants, each up to 30,000 EUR\\nEligibility\\nNon-interventional studies, like retrospective studies or prospective data collection, are eligible for support.\\nApplications for basic research projects are also eligible. In these cases, we favor those that:\\nTake advantage of the collaborative nature of the SWG network\\nEncourage the mutual exchange of expertise\\nTo increase diversity and support inclusion within our SWG community, we encourage proposals that involve:\\nJunior members\\nPartners from underrepresented countries\\nIn all cases, the principal investigator(s) (PIs) must be EHA members and SWG members at the time of application.\\nIndividual applications are not eligible for this call, because each project must foster collaboration and help build a network within the releva\"}, {\"doc_id\": \"web-7ad170c3c8cf26dee84e0e4f\", \"title\": \"Specialized Working Groups - The European Hematology Association (EHA)Specialized Working Groups - The European Hematology Association (EHA)\", \"source_ranges\": [{\"start\": 3922, \"end\": 3948, \"excerpt_start\": 0, \"excerpt_end\": 26}, {\"start\": 4003, \"end\": 5577, \"excerpt_start\": 27, \"excerpt_end\": 1601}], \"navigation_excerpt\": true, \"text\": \"Specialized Working Groups\\nEHA’s Specialized Working Groups (SWGs) foster science and spread knowledge in basic, translational, and clinical research in hematology.\\nTo ensure we cover the full field of hematology and provide a research platform for all groups and researchers, we’ve established two types of SWG:\\nDisease-oriented SWGs—which cover specific topics such as leukemias and lymphomas\\nOverarching SWGs—which cover broad themes such as aging and precision medicine\\nThis model is based on the nine sections of the EHA Roadmap for European Hematology Research.\\nThere are currently 25 SWGs. You can use the filter below to view them by type.\\nHow to join an SWG\\nIf you’re interested in making new connections or sharing your expertise, you can apply to join an SWG. To do this, just follow the instructions on the main page of the relevant SWG.\\nSWG Grants\\nEHA provides financial support to small research projects ini