\nunknown, must not be restated. Repairs may instead use currently offered\nreviewed_observation_ids for immutable sourced rows, under all common source and\nresolution checks. Terminal composition/audit use the ordinary protocol without\nreading_status/search_gaps.\n\nweb_source_review contains exact already-visible originals and read-only links to\nprior judgments/issues/current frozen offers; it adds no evidence or permission.\ndocument_scope_review pairs requested_scopes with each document's earliest visible\noriginal, which may be mid-document. card_original_source_id refers only to this\nrequest's cards[].original_source.id. Establish the requested entity identity and\nevent/relation from offered originals before transferring any attribute. Shared\nsite, partner, topic or question context alone establishes no identity bridge.\nIn an ordinary read, compare new originals with existing requested-slot bindings:\nmatch the requested entity, event/relation and scope before taking an attribute.\nAn associated workflow, version or process can describe a different event.\nknown_observations marked model_judgment are prior model assessments, not semantic\ncertification of their source entailment. Reconsider them against the originals;\ndo not assume either the old or the new statement is correct. If a real conflict\nwarrants correction, use the existing legal contradiction/retract_ids mechanism\nand separate replacement support only where this request permits it. A review\ncard itself never retracts or changes a value. During repair, retain the offered\nedit scope and immutable current-batch rows. Only offered frozen references may\nbe used; each covers its own source_ids only. Support from a new original does\nnot review an old obligated original. Claim an obligation only after warranted\nsettled review of every required original; otherwise leave it pending. Do not\ninvent agreement or call genuine partial/counter evidence irrelevant to close it.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"audit_verdict\":{\"enum\":[\"supported\",\"unknown\",\"conflict\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"final_prediction\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"semantic_answer\":{},\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"allOf\":[{\"else\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"minItems\":1}}}],\"not\":{\"required\":[\"search_gaps\"]}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"reading_status\":{\"const\":\"no_relevant_evidence\"}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"unresolved\":{\"maxItems\":0}}}}],\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"agreement\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"date\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"reading_status\":{\"enum\":[\"no_relevant_evidence\",\"evidence_assessment\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"search_gaps\":{\"description\":\"Untried search directions only; permitted with empty observations and unresolved.\",\"items\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"maxItems\":0,\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"date\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\",\"reading_status\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"web_source_review\":{\"read_only\":true,\"grants_review_authority\":false,\"existing_permissions_unchanged\":true,\"document_scope_review\":{\"requested_scopes\":{\"date\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"documents\":[{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"earliest_visible_original\":{\"id\":\"s68\",\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"start\":10679,\"end\":10912,\"text\":\"For PC members we looked for recognized expertise as demonstrated by strong publication records in quality venues, while taking into account their seniority and research area (which can lead to variations in publication records).    \",\"source_role\":\"document\",\"source_message_indices\":[],\"time_metadata\":{}}}]},\"cards\":[]},\"original_question\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"When was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"output_slot\":\"agreement_date\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"When was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"5600c532ed67fbe686e60b089d2154c6166f4e5ddc6f42e403692c86225a8859\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_joint_memory_v1\",\"program_identity\":\"bc974c3f05127afd571e8b2de0613711721b8d735b15b68fe1b4c4bfaea288f2\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"ed5e830eaf4e6a2a8abd283305d8543b705d810f6a5a03e189ae33c8f6ea234b\"},\"variables\":{\"date\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"agreement_date\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"date\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"date\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"value\",\"result_type\":\"record\",\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\",\"explanation\":\"none\",\"fields\":[\"date\",\"location\"]},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s69\",\"d1\",10912,11312,\"We identified several criteria for selection of the PB/PC: ● Aim for proportional coverage of research areas taking into account the historical number of submissions for each research area (we used the data for distribution of papers to research areas from ICSE 2018)  ● Aim for a good gender balance ● Aim for geographic diversity ● Aim for industry representation ● Include the PC Chairs from ICSE \",\"document\",[]],[\"s68\",\"d1\",10679,10912,\"For PC members we looked for recognized expertise as demonstrated by strong publication records in quality venues, while taking into account their seniority and research area (which can lead to variations in publication records).    \",\"document\",[]],[\"s70\",\"d1\",11312,11483,\"2018 and ICSE 2020 in either the PB or PC ● Aim to satisfy the SIGSOFT policy: For recurring events, at least one-third of the program committee membership should change. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s71\",\"d1\",11483,11805,\"Most program committee members should not serve for more than three consecutive terms and in no instance should an individual serve more than four consecutive terms.  We created a spreadsheet containing information about candidates for PB and PC (such as research areas, organization, country, gender, industry/academia). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s72\",\"d1\",11805,12094,\"We used scripts created by prior ICSE chairs for mining publications in DBLP and prior conference committees to search for overlooked candidates with strong records. We met with the PC chairs of ICSE 2018 and ICSE 2017 to get their input on past performance of PB/PC candidates. Based on \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s73\",\"d1\",12094,12484,\"all the information we gathered and the guidelines we listed above, we created an invitation list and sent the PB invitations in December 2017 and the PC invitations in January 2018. The ICSE 2019 PB and PC were finalized by February 2018.  Of the 132 invitations that were sent out, 21 were either declined or ignored. For these, we added new names, maintaining the criteria given above.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s74\",\"d1\",12484,12812,\"In the final list, the gender balance of the PB was 16F/16M and of the PC was 39F/59M. Statistics on geographic diversity are given in Section 4. Generally, we achieved good geographic diversity, although we could not identify suitable PC/PB members from some countries (most notably India) whilst satisfying other constraints. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s75\",\"d1\",12812,13060,\"It is also instructive to compare the ratio of submitting authors to PB/PC members across countries: such an analysis reveals stark disparities. For example, the ratio for the USA is 11:1 but for China is 108:1. More is said on this in Section 4.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s76\",\"d1\",13060,13437,\"2.d Timeline and the Review Process  In order to meet the PB and PC members, prepare them for the review process, and receive their feedback about the review process, we organized two separate meetings at ICSE 2018, one for the ICSE 2019 PB and one for the ICSE 2019 PC. In these meetings, we went over a set of slides explaining the review process and asked for their input.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s77\",\"d1\",13437,13837,\"We used the same review criteria that were used in recent ICSEs: ● Soundness: How well the paper’s contributions are supported by rigorous application of appropriate research methods, ● Significance: The extent to which the paper’s contributions are novel, original, and important, with respect to the existing body of knowledge, ● Verifiability: Whether the paper includes sufficient information to \",\"document\",[]],[\"s78\",\"d1\",13837,14224,\"support independent verification or replication of the paper’s claimed contributions, ● Presentation: Whether the paper’s quality of writing meets the high standards of ICSE, including clear descriptions and explanations, adequate use of the English language, absence of major ambiguity, clearly readable figures and tables, and adherence to the formatting instructions provided below.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s79\",\"d1\",14224,14496,\"Although the above criteria were listed in the review form, reviewers were not asked to score papers separately on Significance, Soundness, Novelty, Replicability and Presentation Quality. It was considered that these questions did not add value to the decision-making.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s80\",\"d1\",14496,14874,\"Another change in the review form compared to prior years was the addition of a question where reviewers were asked to rank their set of papers - for each paper, to say whether the paper was in the Top 15%, Top 16-30%, Top 31-50%, Bottom 50% of the ICSE submissions. Since no reviewer read all submissions, reviewers were asked to use their batch of submissions as a guideline. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s81\",\"d1\",14874,15027,\"The rationale in adding this question was to get reviewers to self-reflect on any biases in their scoring and help them normalize their recommendations. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s82\",\"d1\",15027,15296,\"For example, a reviewer marking a paper as top 15% but giving a strong reject might reconsider their decision and change either their ranking or the recommendation, or might provide an explanation of why those particular scores make sense during the discussion phase.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s83\",\"d1\",15296,15534,\"Although we did not empirically evaluate the effects of this new question, in at least one instance the incongruence between the ranking and the score given by a reviewer was discussed and this had an influence on the decision making.   \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s84\",\"d1\",15534,15934,\" The review process was based on the following principles:  After the desk rejects: ● All papers received 3 reviews written by PC members ○ More reviewers could be assigned if the expertise among the current reviewers was low ● All papers got a chance at a rebuttal of ALL the reviews ○ This implies that no reviews were conducted after the rebuttal period, or equivalently, all reviews had to be sub\",\"document\",[]],[\"s85\",\"d1\",15934,15970,\"mitted before the rebuttal period.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s86\",\"d1\",15970,16370,\"● All papers received a meta-review, written by the PB members, summarizing the discussion, the reaction to the rebuttal and the decision  ● PB members did not re-review the papers but participated in discussions either as a moderator (discussion lead) or as an additional discussant  After the initial check for violations of submission instructions (which can lead to desk rejection) we carried out\",\"document\",[]],[\"s87\",\"d1\",16370,16770,\" a three phase decision making process: ● Phase 1: After rebuttal, papers with unanimous negative or positive scores were marked as accept or reject as early as possible (we had expected ~60% of papers to be decided this way)  ● Phase 2: Papers with mixed scores were discussed further to reach a consensus decision by the reviewers, under the guidance of the PB member (we had expected ~25-30% of pa\",\"document\",[]],[\"s88\",\"d1\",16770,17060,\"pers to be decided this way) ● Phase 3: Papers for which reviewers were unable to reach a consensus were discussed during the two-day online PB/PC meeting (we had expected ~10-15% of papers to be decided this way)  A high level overview of the review process is shown in the figure below: \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s89\",\"d1\",17060,17443,\" \\n\\nThe actual numbers for different phases of the review process were as follows: ● 529 submissions were received. ● 25 (5%) papers were desk rejected either due to double blind rule violation or due to exceeding the page limit (we applied the page limit strictly; any paper that had technical content beyond page 10 was rejected even if it was a single line beyond the page limit). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s90\",\"d1\",17443,17823,\"● 10 (2%) papers were withdrawn by the authors at various stages of the review process.  ● All remaining 494 papers received at least 3 reviews and one meta-review.   ○ 302 (57%) consensus papers were decided in Phase 1  ○ 192 papers received mixed scores and were discussed in Phase 2. For 127 of these papers (24%) PB and PC members reached a consensus decision before Phase 3. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s91\",\"d1\",17823,18172,\"○ The remaining 65 papers (12%) were discussed in Phase 3. All decisions were finalized by the end of the two-day online PC meeting.  ● 109 (21%) papers were accepted.   It is interesting to note that our predictions about how many papers would be decided in each phase (which we made based on the data from prior years) were reasonably accurate.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s92\",\"d1\",18172,18514,\"We started the online discussions as soon as the reviews were received, although we asked reviewers not to make decisions before the rebuttal period (this differs from 2018 when reviewers were asked - but not mandated - to wait for the author responses). After Phase 2, we assigned two more PB members to each undecided paper as discussants. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s93\",\"d1\",18514,18864,\"Discussant PB members were required to read the papers, all the reviews and the discussions. The role of the discussant was to add an additional perspective on a paper to aid decision-making and to take a view on unresolved issues in the reviews.  During the online meeting (Phase 3) the goal was to reach a decision within the 2 days on all papers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s94\",\"d1\",18864,19251,\"During the online meeting discussion, each paper had at least the following participants: 3 (or more) PC reviewers, a PB discussion leader, and 2 PB discussants. Moreover, the discussions were open to any other PB/PC member with no conflict-of-interest. We empowered the PB members to finalize the decision in cases where reviewers and discussants were unable to converge to a decision. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s95\",\"d1\",19251,19540,\"In practice, consensus was always reached. The PB/PC were instructed that PC Chairs would make an executive decision if a paper remained unresolved at the end of the two-day period. This was not necessary for any papers.  During the online meeting we used Easychair for online discussion. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s96\",\"d1\",19540,19936,\"We occasionally sent emails to the entire PB and PC to answer common questions that came up during discussions. The key issue during the online meeting was to make sure that the PB and PC members were responsive and the discussions were progressing. PC chairs monitored all the ongoing discussions to make sure that progress was being made. We did not use synchronous conversations (skype etc.). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s97\",\"d1\",19936,20300,\"Time difference was a challenge but we do not think it adversely affected the review process.  The ICSE 2019 reviews that were sent to authors did not include the scores entered by the reviewers. Scores were included in the ICSE 2018 reviews sent to authors but not in prior \\nyears. Some authors raised strong objections to scores being excluded from the reviews. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s98\",\"d1\",20300,20665,\"Authors complained about the lack of scores in the reviews since they believed that having the scores helps them to write a better response to reviewers and to make a decision about whether they should withdraw the paper. We told authors that we view the reviewers' scores as part of the PB/PC discussions (like the online comments reviewers post about the paper). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s99\",\"d1\",20665,20989,\"In their response, we encouraged the authors to focus on the substance of the reviews and the explicit questions provided by the reviewers. Sending review scores has become common in conferences, so author expectations may be changing due to this. Future chairs should consider the pros and cons of sending review scores.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s100\",\"d1\",20989,21389,\"One minor issue we faced during the process was that in preparation of the meta-reviews PB members sometimes upload “dummy” reviews to indicate which direction the decision is going, to be updated later when they had time to enter a complete meta-review. In one case, a PB member forgot to update the “dummy” meta-review and important feedback summarizing the discussion was not sent to the authors. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s101\",\"d1\",21389,21788,\"We eventually noticed this and sent the information to the authors.  In another case, we received a complaint from authors of a paper that some comments in a review were discriminatory against non-native English speakers. We let the PC member know that it is not appropriate to speculate if an author is or is not a native English speaker in a review.  The PC member promptly revised the review.    \",\"document\",[]],[\"s102\",\"d1\",21788,22171,\"Given that the workload is significant for the PB and PC and the reviews and meta-reviews are written under time pressure, it is crucial for PB/PC members to make a “sanity check” pass on all their reviews and meta-reviews before they are sent to authors.  We did not make early reject decisions in ICSE 2019 other than desk reject decisions which were sent to authors in September. \",\"document\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"source_documents\":{\"d1\":{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"time_metadata\":{}}},\"source_span_columns\":[\"id\",\"doc_id\",\"start\",\"end\",\"text\",\"source_role\",\"source_message_indices\"]}"}], "logical_call_index": 16, "response_format": "plain", "input_tokens_local": 7154, "output_token_reserve": 4096, "input_margin": 256, "max_model_len": 120000, "tokenization_request_sha256": "953a49d37fa5f06e7e9ffa7d081894a3b3314454d66e4588029195b40c79f5a9", "tokenization_endpoint_sha256": "0ed12ee94b55bfdbc62fb247243af2d71aa1d7ea503f5f191605ae295c5ad94a", "token_count_is_estimate": false, "token_estimator_id": "deployment_vllm_chat_v1", "tokenizer_id": null, "add_generation_prompt": true, "tokenization_scope": "adapted_plain_text_chat_default_server_template", "token_accounting_note": "Deployment input count; model weights and tokenizer hash are not independently verified", "tokenization_wire_sha256": "c6e40561114e337ce4372873e93570563e70958b1035f7ea18ff1255091cd73c", "raw_output": "```json\n{\n  \"slots\": [\n    {\n      \"variable\": \"date\",\n      \"observations\": [],\n      \"unresolved\": [],\n      \"reading_status\": \"no_relevant_evidence\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```", "response_metadata": {"finish_reason": "stop", "refusal": null, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 7154, "total_tokens": 7205, "completion_tokens": 51, "prompt_tokens_details": null, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": null, "accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null}}, "re