ions' source_ids must cover EVERY source_ids\nentry of the offered obligation. Review coverage is not a claim that every source\nis a premise of a support: genuinely irrelevant or corrective original text may\nhave a separate sourced review. Never add unsupported citations, classify text\nautomatically, or label supporting evidence irrelevant merely to satisfy coverage.\nAn empty slot does not resolve an earlier issue. Do not assert hypothetical\nwhole-corpus exhaustiveness requirements; scoped sufficient evidence may resolve\na missing-evidence issue without having read every memory.\n\nWhen source_span_columns is present, source_spans contains row arrays. Match each\nrow position to source_span_columns to reconstruct every span object before\nreading. References to source_spans[].id or other fields mean those objects.\nThe id column holds source aliases (sN); doc_id holds the original document ID or,\nwhen source_documents is present, its metadata reference (dN), never source_ids.\n\nWEB SOURCE CHECK: exact entity/event/relation needs originals; audit them.\nPublication/revision/effective/event dates differ; people/orgs aren't places.\nMenu/topic labels and adjacent text prove no relation.\nAnother project's dates cannot transfer without source proof.\npartial/unknown: real incomplete evidence within the requested scope.\nEmpty ordinary retrieval uses\nno_relevant_evidence. Existing obligations still require legal model review of originals.\n\nREQUIRED WEB RETRIEVAL PROTOCOL: every requested slot declares reading_status;\nBOTH statuses require unresolved=[]. no_relevant_evidence means NO NEW admissible\nobservation: observations=[], no review/resolution IDs. Optional search_gaps lists\nuntried directions, only with this status. Old facts/obligations stay unchanged in\ncontroller state; no need to repeat them. This search result is never a negative\nfact or proof of absence. Do not cite an answer-free window as unknown; continue\nthe local bridge. evidence_assessment supplies NEW original-source-cited ordinary\nobservations. A genuine incomplete statement/ambiguity/conflict requires a named\npartial/unknown row with exact source_ids, requested scope and reason; unknown\nuses value=null. Never replace it with bare slot.unresolved or a resolution claim.\nOld evidence obligations, failed drafts and counters remain mandatory. To settle\nan offered issue, use evidence_assessment, explicitly review its original text\nwith sourced support/corrective/irrelevant observations, cover EVERY required\noriginal source, and claim the offered resolution ID. Genuinely irrelevant text\nmay settle an earlier absence-only issue; never relabel real partial facts,\nsupporting text or counters irrelevant just to close them. Empty slots and\npartial/unknown observations cannot settle evidence obligations: omit their IDs\nfrom resolved_obligation_ids. Correcting a failed draft need not settle a source\nissue. Preserve every required failed draft ID. Frozen rows, including partial/\nunknown, must not be restated. Repairs may instead use currently offered\nreviewed_observation_ids for immutable sourced rows, under all common source and\nresolution checks. Terminal composition/audit use the ordinary protocol without\nreading_status/search_gaps.\n\nweb_source_review contains exact already-visible originals and read-only links to\nprior judgments/issues/current frozen offers; it adds no evidence or permission.\ndocument_scope_review pairs requested_scopes with each document's earliest visible\noriginal, which may be mid-document. card_original_source_id refers only to this\nrequest's cards[].original_source.id. Establish the requested entity identity and\nevent/relation from offered originals before transferring any attribute. Shared\nsite, partner, topic or question context alone establishes no identity bridge.\nIn an ordinary read, compare new originals with existing requested-slot bindings:\nmatch the requested entity, event/relation and scope before taking an attribute.\nAn associated workflow, version or process can describe a different event.\nknown_observations marked model_judgment are prior model assessments, not semantic\ncertification of their source entailment. Reconsider them against the originals;\ndo not assume either the old or the new statement is correct. If a real conflict\nwarrants correction, use the existing legal contradiction/retract_ids mechanism\nand separate replacement support only where this request permits it. A review\ncard itself never retracts or changes a value. During repair, retain the offered\nedit scope and immutable current-batch rows. Only offered frozen references may\nbe used; each covers its own source_ids only. Support from a new original does\nnot review an old obligated original. Claim an obligation only after warranted\nsettled review of every required original; otherwise leave it pending. Do not\ninvent agreement or call genuine partial/counter evidence irrelevant to close it.\n\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"audit_verdict\":{\"enum\":[\"supported\",\"unknown\",\"conflict\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"final_prediction\":{\"type\":[\"string\",\"null\"]},\"semantic_answer\":{},\"slots\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"allOf\":[{\"else\":{\"anyOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"minItems\":1}}}],\"not\":{\"required\":[\"search_gaps\"]}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"reading_status\":{\"const\":\"no_relevant_evidence\"}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"unresolved\":{\"maxItems\":0}}}}],\"oneOf\":[{\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"allOf\":[{\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\"]}}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"const\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\"},\"time_scope\":{\"const\":\"agreement\"}}}}],\"else\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"type\":\"null\"}}},\"if\":{\"properties\":{\"stance\":{\"const\":\"support\"}}},\"properties\":{\"retract_ids\":{\"maxItems\":0},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"]}},\"then\":{\"properties\":{\"value\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}}}}},\"variable\":{\"const\":\"location\"}}}],\"properties\":{\"observations\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"entity_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"parent_versions\":{\"additionalProperties\":{\"type\":\"integer\"},\"type\":\"object\"},\"reason\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"retract_ids\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"source_ids\":{\"description\":\"Exact visible source_spans[].id aliases, such as s3; never document IDs or invented IDs.\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"stance\":{\"enum\":[\"support\",\"contradiction\",\"partial\",\"unknown\",\"irrelevant\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"time_scope\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"value\":{}},\"required\":[\"id\",\"stance\",\"value\",\"source_ids\",\"entity_scope\",\"time_scope\",\"parent_versions\",\"retract_ids\",\"reason\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"type\":\"array\"},\"reading_status\":{\"enum\":[\"no_relevant_evidence\",\"evidence_assessment\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"search_gaps\":{\"description\":\"Untried search directions only; permitted with empty observations and unresolved.\",\"items\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"},\"unresolved\":{\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"maxItems\":0,\"type\":\"array\"},\"variable\":{\"enum\":[\"location\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"variable\",\"observations\",\"unresolved\",\"reading_status\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":1,\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"slots\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"web_source_review\":{\"read_only\":true,\"grants_review_authority\":false,\"existing_permissions_unchanged\":true,\"document_scope_review\":{\"requested_scopes\":{\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"documents\":[{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"earliest_visible_original\":{\"id\":\"s102\",\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"start\":21788,\"end\":22171,\"text\":\"Given that the workload is significant for the PB and PC and the reviews and meta-reviews are written under time pressure, it is crucial for PB/PC members to make a “sanity check” pass on all their reviews and meta-reviews before they are sent to authors.  We did not make early reject decisions in ICSE 2019 other than desk reject decisions which were sent to authors in September. \",\"source_role\":\"document\",\"source_message_indices\":[],\"time_metadata\":{}}}]},\"cards\":[]},\"original_question\":\"When and where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"task\":{\"question\":\"Where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"output_slot\":\"agreement_location\",\"answer_type\":\"text\",\"inputs\":[],\"execution\":\"retrieval\"},\"grounded_subquestion\":\"Where was the ICSE duplicate submission statement agreed upon?\",\"request_context\":{\"query_identity\":\"46754e5d1bfe94f3069272571aa2de1e4f4ae699beb1c08a82251f9d94756933\",\"algorithm_version\":\"dagbt_joint_memory_v1\",\"program_identity\":\"bc974c3f05127afd571e8b2de0613711721b8d735b15b68fe1b4c4bfaea288f2\",\"initial_dag_identity\":\"ed5e830eaf4e6a2a8abd283305d8543b705d810f6a5a03e189ae33c8f6ea234b\"},\"variables\":{\"location\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"demand\":\"agreement_location\",\"open\":true,\"scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}}},\"requested_slots\":[\"location\"],\"requested_scopes\":{\"location\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"}},\"output_contract\":{\"mode\":\"value\",\"result_type\":\"record\",\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\",\"explanation\":\"none\",\"fields\":[\"date\",\"location\"]},\"program_revision\":0,\"rule_binding\":null,\"supported_parents\":{},\"known_observations\":[],\"source_spans\":[[\"s103\",\"d1\",22171,22263,\"For the remaining submissions, the notifications were sent on December 12th as announced.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s102\",\"d1\",21788,22171,\"Given that the workload is significant for the PB and PC and the reviews and meta-reviews are written under time pressure, it is crucial for PB/PC members to make a “sanity check” pass on all their reviews and meta-reviews before they are sent to authors.  We did not make early reject decisions in ICSE 2019 other than desk reject decisions which were sent to authors in September. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s104\",\"d1\",22263,22663,\"The timeline of the review process was as follows:  TASK DATES submission deadline Aug 24 (Fri) bidding + COI Aug 25-Aug 29 (Sat-Wed) desk rejects Sep 3-4 (Mon-Tue) paper assignments Aug 30-Sep 5 (Thu-Wed) 1st round reviews Sep 26 (Wed) 2nd round reviews Oct 17 (Wed) review assessment & improvement Sep 26-Nov 7 (Wed-Wed) additional review requests Oct 18-23 (Thu-Tue) additional paper assignments O\",\"document\",[]],[\"s105\",\"d1\",22663,22818,\"ct 24-25 (Wed-Thu) additional reviews Oct 26-Nov 2 (Fri-Fri) rebuttal Nov 12-14 (Mon-Wed) online discussion Sep 26-Nov 30 (Fri) consensus accept & reject? \",\"document\",[]],[\"s106\",\"d1\",22818,23098,\"Nov 19 (Mon)  pre meeting accept & reject Nov 26 (Mon) \\nassign extra PB members as discussant Nov 26-27 (Mon-Tue) online meeting Dec 3-4 (Mon-Tue) author notification Dec 12   Note that we provided a table showing a detailed list of deadlines for both the PB and PC (Appendix B). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s107\",\"d1\",23098,23438,\"The link for this was provided as a reminder in all email communications with the PB/PC: it served as an easily accessible source of information on upcoming deadlines and helped to keep everyone on track. Informal feedback has been that this resource was very well received and so we encourage future PC Chairs to adopt a similar practice. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s108\",\"d1\",23438,23785,\"We also provided a link to a FAQ page (Appendix C), which we updated as the review process evolved.   2.e Conference Program  The General Chair and PC Co-Chairs decided to introduce a “blended” program for ICSE 2019 where papers from the Technical, SEIP, NIER, Journal First, and Demonstration tracks were combined in sessions organized by topic. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s109\",\"d1\",23785,24138,\"The program consisted of 90 or 120 minutes long blended sessions. Presentation times were allocated for different tracks as follows: ● Technical track papers: 20 minutes ● SEIP track papers: 20 minutes ● Demonstration track papers: 20 minutes ● NIER track papers: 10 minutes ● Journal first track papers: 10 minutes  These timings include time for Q&A. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s110\",\"d1\",24138,24480,\"For 20 minute presentations we recommended 15-17 minute talks followed by 1 or 2 questions from the audience. For 10 minute presentations we recommended 8 minute talks followed by 1 or 2 questions from the audience. Most sessions were followed with a 10 minute discussion period for the audience to ask more detailed questions of presenters. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s111\",\"d1\",24480,24804,\"We invited the authors to bring a poster about their work to the presentation room to facilitate discussions during the discussion period at the end of the session.   The program also designated some papers as more industrially relevant. These papers were marked in the conference program as part of the “Industry Program”. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s112\",\"d1\",24804,25110,\"The decision on which papers to include was informed by an additional question in the review form asking PC members to mark industrially-relevant papers. Due to scheduling constraints, some Industry Program sessions also included papers not so marked - based on the General Chair/PC Chairs’ best judgment. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s113\",\"d1\",25110,25397,\"One of the three ICSE keynotes was a SEIP keynote and also advertised as part of the Industry Program  2.f Awards  ACM Distinguished Paper Award Selection ACM allows ICSE to select up to 10% of accepted papers as award papers. Rounding up, this leads to up to 11 awards for ICSE 2019.  \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s114\",\"d1\",25397,25761,\"ACM partially stipulates a process for selecting awardees, namely: “The program committee will take a weighted vote, respecting the conflict of interest rules in place for the conference, to identify the top candidates among the papers. The program committee chair(s) will use the results of the weighted votes as a primary basis for selecting the award papers.”  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s115\",\"d1\",25761,26090,\"To make decisions on award papers, we applied the following process.  1. The review form included a question asking reviewers if they wanted to nominate the paper for the award. Of the 1487 reviews we received, 42 of them nominated a paper for the award. Of these 42, only 4 received a nomination from more than one reviewer. 2. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s116\",\"d1\",26090,26385,\"We selected a longlist of 15 papers from these 42, according to the following criterion: at least one reviewer nominates the paper for an award AND there are no negative scores in the reviews.  3. We conducted a poll of the PB/PC asking them to vote for up to three papers from the longlist. 4. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s117\",\"d1\",26385,26734,\"Based on the vote and a careful reading of the reviews and online discussion, 11 papers were selected to receive an award.  There appears to be no standard process for selecting Best Paper awardees at ICSE. Rather, each year, the PC Chairs use their best judgment to decide on a process. It may be worth the ICSE SC introducing a standard process.  \",\"document\",[]],[\"s118\",\"d1\",26734,27131,\"Distinguished Reviewer Awards PB and PC members are volunteers who spend a significant amount of time and effort during the review process. We believe that it is worthwhile to acknowledge the efforts of reviewers who do an outstanding job. For this purpose, we asked PB members to nominate PC members for the Distinguished Reviewer Award. PB members were asked to apply the following criteria: 1. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s119\",\"d1\",27131,27424,\"The reviewer consistently wrote high quality reviews that: ○ Gave clear feedback to authors in terms of soundness, significance, verifiability and presentation ○ Gave clear recommendations for accept/reject decisions ○ Did so in a professional manner using polite and constructive language 2. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s120\",\"d1\",27424,27690,\"The reviewer consistently participated in online discussions in a way that was responsive, constructive, collaborative and clear  32 PC members were nominated to receive an award. Most of these were nominated only once (26) with the others nominated multiple times. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s121\",\"d1\",27690,27943,\"These 6 were all selected to receive an award; in addition, the PC Chairs added 2 names from the remaining list based on a careful reading of all the reviews from the nominees. Hence, 8 members of the PC received a Distinguished Reviewer Award.        \\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s122\",\"d1\",27943,28319,\"3 Discussion on Key Aspects of the Review Process  3.a Double Blind Reviewing  ICSE 2019 followed the “heavy” double-blind review model where names of the authors are not revealed until the final decisions (for accepted papers) or never (for rejected papers). The same review model was used in ICSE 2018.   Checking submissions for double-blind violations can be challenging. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s123\",\"d1\",28319,28528,\"The PC chairs and PB members checked papers for double blind violations. Some papers were desk rejected due to egregious double blind violations such as listing the authors’ names with the title of the paper. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s124\",\"d1\",28528,28897,\"Since double-blind reviewing is rather new to ICSE, we decided not to desk reject minor violations such as listing of grants in the acknowledgements that reveal author identities, providing a link to a repository where the user name of the owner is visible, blacking out names of authors in the pdf file (author names can be seen by copy-pasting the blacked out text). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s125\",\"d1\",28897,29241,\"Future PC chairs may decide to apply the double-blind rule more strictly as the ICSE author community becomes more familiar with the process. It would also be helpful to provide some guidelines about how to prevent some common mistakes in anonymizing papers such as, incorrect anonymization of a web link, a tool repository, a pdf file, etc.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s126\",\"d1\",29241,29577,\"We instructed the reviewers not to actively search for submissions online and in general avoid activities that would deanonymize the paper. In some cases reviewers discovered the author identity for various reasons (they already reviewed an earlier version of the work, or they knew about the research as it was being developed, etc.). \",\"document\",[]],[\"s127\",\"d1\",29577,29908,\"We did not reassign papers to other reviewers in cases where the author identities were inadvertently discovered by the reviewers. Our assessment was that the review process should not be disrupted to achieve perfect anonymity since there will always be cases where the reviewers can make an informed guess who the authors may be. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s128\",\"d1\",29908,30282,\"We think that the costs and benefits of achieving better anonymity of the authors will continue to be an issue that future PC chairs will need to take into account.   We provided a Q&A page to clarify the rules for double blind submissions (which was a revised version of the Q&A page from ICSE 2018). However, we still received many questions about double blind reviewing. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s129\",\"d1\",30282,30640,\"One issue authors struggle with is how to reference their prior work. The answer is to cite the prior work in third person. But this may not be possible if the prior work is not published (for example it could be a dissertation). We advised authors to omit citations to their unpublished work and add such citations in the camera-ready version of the paper. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s130\",\"d1\",30640,30936,\"The important thing to communicate to authors is that the double-blind rule should never be used as a justification for not citing prior published work.  Some authors were concerned about how citing a previously published short paper by them (such as a NIER paper) would influence the reviewers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s131\",\"d1\",30936,31173,\"Authors believed that if the reviewers knew that the authors of the short paper and the full paper are the same, they would be less concerned about the significance of the contribution (delta) between the short paper and the full paper. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s132\",\"d1\",31173,31527,\"We argue that the author identity should not matter in this scenario, but this is a scenario worth discussing with the reviewers since people may have differing opinions on this issue.  \\nSeveral authors asked when they could post the paper to an online repository (such as arXiv.org). We advised the authors to wait until the submission of the rebuttal. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s133\",\"d1\",31527,31876,\"Given the time it takes for the ICSE review process to conclude, it is reasonable for authors to request distribution of their work online before they receive the decision about their submission. In some cases the authors’ organizations may want to advertise the work and in other cases authors may worry about being “scooped” by other researchers. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s134\",\"d1\",31876,32177,\"We believe that this issue will continue to be a problem for double-blind. We believe that a double blind review process should not prevent sharing of ideas, so it is important to balance the desire to have an unbiased review process with the ability of authors to share their research with others.   \",\"document\",[]],[\"s135\",\"d1\",32177,32475,\"3.b Artifact Submission and Evaluation  One change in ICSE 2019 (compared to ICSE 2018) was that authors were not allowed to submit supplementary artifacts simultaneously with the paper submission. Instead, artifact submission and evaluation was handled separately by an Artifact Evaluation Track. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s136\",\"d1\",32475,32875,\"Authors of papers accepted to the Technical Track were invited to submit an artifact to the Artifact Track. The Artifact Evaluation committee reviewed the artifacts and classified the accepted artifacts as Functional, Reusable, Available, Replicated, or Reproduced. Artifacts that were classified as Reusable, Available, Replicated or Reproduced received badges in the program and the proceedings.   \",\"document\",[]]],\"required_scope\":{\"entity_scope\":\"ICSE duplicate submission statement\",\"time_scope\":\"agreement\"},\"rules_completion_required\":false,\"supported_task_outputs\":{},\"source_documents\":{\"d1\":{\"doc_id\":\"web-f2480c9be1232e0ef2ee3058\",\"time_metadata\":{}}},\"source_span_columns\":[\"id\",\"doc_id\",\"start\",\"end\",\"text\",\"source_role\",\"source_message_indices\"]}"}], "logical_call_index": 20, "response_format": "plain", "input_tokens_local": 7141, "output_token_reserve": 4096, "input_margin": 256, "max_model_len": 120000, "tokenization_request_sha256": "dc3e4254e800286edeff2302a63a638e89a3fffbff00d58c750f8bb71ef1f370", "tokenization_endpoint_sha256": "0ed12ee94b55bfdbc62fb247243af2d71aa1d7ea503f5f191605ae295c5ad94a", "token_count_is_estimate": false, "token_estimator_id": "deployment_vllm_chat_v1", "tokenizer_id": null, "add_generation_prompt": true, "to